# Line-ending policy.
#
# This file is committed, so it travels with the repo and OVERRIDES whatever
# core.autocrlf each machine happens to have set. That is the point: every
# development machine, and any fresh clone, agree without local configuration.

# Everything text: LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
# Not just "LF when committed" — eol=lf also stops checkout rewriting to CRLF,
# which keeps WSL happy, since WSL and Windows share the same working-tree files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Determinism-critical data: NEVER translate.
#
# The replay test compares event logs BYTE FOR BYTE, and that comparison is the
# evidence for the project's central claim that the core is deterministic. The test
# compares two in-memory runs, so no golden files are committed; these rules protect
# golden logs or test data if any are added. A line-ending conversion in that path
# would produce diffs that have nothing to do with the engine, and only intermittently
# (after a fresh clone, or on one machine only): the worst possible failure mode for
# the one test whose job is to be trustworthy.
#
# `-text` disables eol conversion but keeps textual diffs readable, which
# `binary` would not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.golden        -text
tests/golden/** -text
tests/data/**   -text

# Scripts must be LF or Linux refuses them: /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter
*.sh text eol=lf

# Genuinely binary
*.exe binary
*.o   binary
*.obj binary
